{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794133965,"nonce":"BuoUPRFa3ogN8ixtyip/UImcWoI=","message":"Update to the earlier note, landed in c17aa9837b0e34a84e9a43b0333d9ff0b95b9cf5. The comma limit is gone: a quoted family may hold a comma. The parser splits the list at commas outside quotes and writes such a name in double quotes in the stack (backslash and quote escaped), and the registry's family_stack reads it back whole. A quoted keyword is a family name, not the generic (\"serif\" names a family called serif), as in CSS. Remaining limit: per-glyph fallback along the stack is not done (browsers try the next family for a glyph the first lacks); the existing CJK and emoji script fallbacks still apply. Tests are the same as listed before plus a quoted-comma case in the parser and the registry; green on macOS, the Ubuntu NUC (stack tests), nextest on css, text, layout, theme, cn, core and the recorder suite.","files":null}]}